{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1785166867,"nonce":"n8dsd77l4rX0521bqwGWtL3dHOc=","message":"Fix built on feature/has-newer-first-class (pushed to origin), sent to Mark for testing:\nhttps://lore.kernel.org/tools/20260727-innocent-zippy-sambar-51cfe4@meerkat/\n\nThe reported blocker is removed: upgrade is no longer gated on status, so a\n'partial' series can upgrade to a newer revision directly -- no more\napply-then-revert dance to unstick it (Step 2, 050cc4d).\n\nCaveat on the root cause from comment #1: the 'partial' label itself can\nstill linger after a revert, because it is derived from persistent 'taken'\nmarkers and a revert leaves the original commit in history. This was left\ndeliberately un-reconciled -- auto-clearing on rescan can't detect a revert\nreliably and would reintroduce exactly the behind-the-user's-back status\nmutation this series set out to remove. An explicit 'reset coverage' action\nis the clean follow-up if the stale label proves annoying in practice.","files":null}]}